Now I have the full picture. This is an MSTest 4.x integration test suite on .NET 10 with the `[TestCategory("Integration")]` marker. Let me compile the formal audit.

---

## testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Audit

**Target:** `DataAccess.IntegrationTests/UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`
**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10 · 8 test methods
**Integration calibration:** Applied — class is annotated `[TestCategory("Integration")]`

---

### 1. Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 2           | 2              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **4**       | **4**          |

4 of 8 test methods (50%) exhibit at least one smell. The other 4 are clean.

---

### 2. Findings by Severity

#### ❌ HIGH — Conditional Test Logic (testsmells.org Smell #1)

**Test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 93)

The `if`/`else` branch means only one assertion path ever executes per run. If `loaded` is never a `PremiumUser`, the discount-rate assertion is dead code — a hidden testing gap.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — branching changes which assertion runs
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

**Fix:** Split into two focused tests, each controlling the input to guarantee its path:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsInstanceOfType<PremiumUser>(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is likely never tested, giving false coverage confidence.

---

#### ❌ HIGH — Assertion-Free Test / Unknown Test (testsmells.org Smell #4)

**Test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

Inserts 100 users but never asserts anything. MSTest will report this as "passed" even if every insert silently fails or corrupts data.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — no assertion; passes unconditionally unless an exception is thrown
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
}
```

> **Calibration note:** The method name implies a "does not throw" intent, which is a legitimate (if weak) contract. Even so, asserting on the outcome is strictly better.

**Fix:** Assert on the observable effect of the bulk insert:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage looks good but nothing is actually verified.

---

#### ⚠️ MEDIUM — Sleepy Test (testsmells.org Smell #3)

**Test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

Hard-codes a 3-second `Thread.Sleep`. This adds 3 seconds to every test run, is non-deterministic (may be too short under CI load), and blocks a thread-pool thread in an `async` method.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — synchronous block in an async method, hard 3 s wait
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    Thread.Sleep(3000);  // <-- sleepy test

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
}
```

> **Integration calibration:** Downgraded from High → Medium because integration tests sometimes genuinely wait for async side-effects. The smell persists, though, because a polling/retry approach is more reliable and faster.

**Fix:** Replace with a polling helper or, if the notification is event-driven, await the event directly:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll with timeout instead of sleeping
    var sent = await WaitUntilAsync(
        () => _repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

    Assert.IsTrue(sent, "Notification was not sent within the expected time.");
}

private static async Task<bool> WaitUntilAsync(Func<bool> condition, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow + timeout;
    while (DateTime.UtcNow < deadline)
    {
        if (condition()) return true;
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
    return false;
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures and a 3 s tax on every run. `Thread.Sleep` in an `async` method also blocks a thread-pool thread unnecessarily.

---

#### ⚠️ MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette (testsmells.org Extended Smell)

**Test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28)

Contains 4 assertions, none with a message parameter. When one fails, the MSTest output shows only the assertion type and values — the developer must read the source to determine which property was wrong.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — 4 bare assertions, no messages
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);
```

**Fix:** Add message parameters so failures are self-describing:

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Name should round-trip through the database.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Email should round-trip through the database.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Database should assign a positive auto-increment ID.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower diagnosis of CI failures, especially when you can't reproduce locally.

---

### 3. Smell-Free Patterns ✅

These 4 tests are **clean** and well-structured — they demonstrate good integration-test design:

| Test | Why it's good |
|------|---------------|
| `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord` | Clear Arrange/Act/Assert, focused assertions, tests one behavior |
| `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` | Negative assertion (`Assert.IsNull`) verifies absence — clean |
| `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` | `Assert.AreEqual(3, ...)` is self-documenting (3 items just inserted), `CollectionAssert` is idiomatic |
| `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` | Proper async/await, concurrency is the explicit focus, clear count assertion |

The class-level setup (`[TestInitialize]`/`[TestCleanup]` with in-memory SQLite) is a good pattern — every field is used by every test (**no** General Fixture smell), and the in-memory database avoids Mystery Guest / Resource Optimism.

---

### 4. Smells Not Detected (15 of 19 clean)

| Smell | Status |
|-------|--------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean — hermetic in-memory SQLite |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean — no test calls 4+ distinct production methods (integration-calibrated) |
| Magic Number Test | ✅ Clean — all literals are contextually obvious |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `.ToString()` comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean — no try/catch blocks |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean — both fields used by all 8 tests |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` attributes |
